  "passages": [
    {
      "id": "b2.health-and-psychology.new-routine",
      "level": "B2",
      "topics": ["health-and-psychology"],
      "title": "A new routine",
      "text": "When Marta started working from home, she thought her stress would disappear: no more traffic and no more noisy office. But after a few months she felt anxious most of the time, and her mood changed quickly. She worked late every night because her office was also her living room, and she was close to burnout. A therapist helped her understand the problem. Our bodies are used to clear signals that tell them when work starts and ends, and Marta had lost all of them. Together they made a plan. Marta now gets dressed every morning as if she were going out, takes a short walk before she starts and closes her laptop at six o'clock. \"At first it felt silly,\" she says, \"but I got used to it in a couple of weeks.\" She also started talking openly about mental health with her colleagues and discovered that many of them were coping with the same feelings. Psychologists say that small daily habits protect our well-being better than big changes we cannot keep. \"I wish I had asked for help earlier,\" Marta admits. \"It isn't a weakness. It's part of looking after yourself.\"",
      "source": {
        "kind": "original"
      }
    }
  ],
